
# Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks
[colab]: https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1N9gmXIGNU-lRnyaCCMwCNab4AQu5NnUF?usp=sharing

![](/assets/frontier/kan.png)

I have been playing with some implementations of Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks
(KANs), the architecture Liu et al. proposed in April 2024. The pitch is
simple and a bit provocative: take an MLP, move the nonlinearities from the
nodes to the edges, and make them _learnable_. Every weight becomes a small
univariate function, parameterized as a spline. The paper claimed better
accuracy per parameter than MLPs, faster neural scaling laws, and networks you
can read off as symbolic formulas.

In general, the promising ideas shown in the original paper are not
so easy to replicate. I personally believe that KANs are a promising
research direction, but the hype around the original paper created
an unrealistic expectation of what KANs can do for now.

In this post I want to go through the whole thing properly: the theorem the
name comes from, how the architecture relaxes it, how the B-spline edges
actually work (with a widget you can drag), a from-scratch KAN in NumPy that
runs in your browser against an MLP, and finally where I think KANs stand.
For the original exploration, here is the colab where I implemented the
models [Link to code][colab].

---

## The Kolmogorov-Arnold representation theorem

In 1957 Kolmogorov, building on work by his student Arnold on Hilbert's
13th problem, proved a remarkable statement about continuous functions of
several variables.

**Theorem (Kolmogorov-Arnold).** Let $f : [0,1]^n \to \mathbb{R}$ be
continuous. Then there exist continuous univariate functions
$\phi_{q,p} : [0,1] \to \mathbb{R}$ and $\Phi_q : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$
such that

$$
f(x_1, \dots, x_n) = \sum_{q=0}^{2n} \Phi_q\left( \sum_{p=1}^{n} \phi_{q,p}(x_p) \right).
$$

Moreover, the inner functions $\phi_{q,p}$ can be chosen once and for all,
independently of $f$. Only the outer functions $\Phi_q$ depend on the target.

Read that again, because it is strange. Every continuous multivariate function
is a finite composition of univariate functions and addition. The only truly
"multivariate" operation you ever need is $+$. In some sense, there are no
genuinely multivariate continuous functions.

So why didn't this become the foundation of neural networks decades ago?
Because the catch is in the regularity. The inner functions guaranteed by the
theorem can be extremely rough, fractal-like objects. They are not
something you can learn with gradient descent or even write down nicely.
Girosi and Poggio made exactly this argument in 1989, in a paper whose
title is the whole argument: _Kolmogorov's theorem is irrelevant_. For decades
that was the consensus.

### How KANs relax the theorem

The theorem has a fixed shape: $n$ inputs, a hidden layer of $2n+1$ sums,
one output, and depth two. Liu et al. read this as a two-layer network,
$[n, 2n+1, 1]$, where every _edge_ carries a univariate function and every
_node_ just sums its inputs. Then they do the deep learning thing: forget
about the exact shape and stack such layers, as wide and as deep as you like.

A KAN layer with $n_l$ inputs and $n_{l+1}$ outputs is a
matrix of $n_l \times n_{l+1}$ learnable functions
$\phi_{j,i}$:

$$
x^{(l+1)}_j = \sum_{i=1}^{n_l} \phi^{(l)}_{j,i}\left(x^{(l)}_i\right),
\qquad
\text{KAN}(x) = \left(\Phi^{(L-1)} \circ \cdots \circ \Phi^{(1)} \circ \Phi^{(0)}\right)(x).
$$

Compare this with an MLP layer:

$$
x^{(l+1)} = \sigma\left(W^{(l)} x^{(l)} + b^{(l)}\right).
$$

In an MLP the edges are scalars, linear and learnable, while the nonlinearity
$\sigma$ sits on the nodes and is _fixed_. In a KAN the edges are nonlinear and
learnable, and the nodes only add. The bet is that with deep stacks you no
longer need the pathological inner functions of the original theorem, and that
smooth functions with compositional structure get _smooth_ KAN
representations. That bet is a heuristic, not a theorem. The paper does prove
an approximation bound, but it assumes the target already has a smooth
KAN representation.

A small observation that helped me: since every $\phi$ is a linear
combination of fixed basis functions (next section), a KAN layer is a linear
layer acting on a _fixed per-input feature expansion_
$x_i \mapsto (B_0(x_i), \dots, B_{G+k-1}(x_i))$. The learnable part is still
just a matrix. That is why the implementation below is mostly `einsum`.

---

## B-spline edges

Each edge function is parameterized as

$$
\phi(x) = w_b \, \text{silu}(x) + \sum_{m=0}^{G+k-1} c_m B_m(x),
\qquad
\text{silu}(x) = \frac{x}{1 + e^{-x}}.
$$

The **grid** is a set of $G$ equal intervals on the expected input range, say
$[-1, 1]$, with knots $t_0 < t_1 < \dots$ spaced $h = 2/G$ apart. The grid is
extended by $k$ extra knots on each side, so that every point of $[-1,1]$
is covered by the same number of basis functions.

The **basis functions** $B_m$ are B-splines of order $k$ (the paper uses
$k = 3$, cubic), defined by the Cox-de Boor recursion:

$$
B_{m,0}(x) = \begin{cases} 1 & t_m \le x < t_{m+1} \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}
$$

$$
B_{m,p}(x) = \frac{x - t_m}{t_{m+p} - t_m} B_{m,p-1}(x)
+ \frac{t_{m+p+1} - x}{t_{m+p+1} - t_{m+1}} B_{m+1,p-1}(x).
$$

Each $B_{m,k}$ is a piecewise polynomial of degree $k$, nonzero only on the
$k+1$ intervals $[t_m, t_{m+k+1})$, and on the grid they sum to one. Two
properties matter for learning. **Local support**: changing $c_m$ only moves
$\phi$ on those $k+1$ intervals, so different regions of the input are learned
by different parameters. **Smoothness**: cubic splines are $C^2$, so the
function and its first two derivatives are continuous and gradients behave.

The **residual base term** $w_b \, \text{silu}(x)$ is the paper's version of a
skip connection. It gives every edge a reasonable global shape from
initialization, and it keeps the edge alive outside the grid, where every
B-spline is zero. The original implementation also has a separate scale $w_s$
multiplying the spline. That is redundant with the $c_m$, so I fold it in.

**Grid refinement.** A trained spline on a grid of $G$ intervals can be
transferred to a finer grid of $2G$ intervals by least squares. Because the
coarse knots are a subset of the fine ones, the coarse spline space is a
subspace of the fine one, and the transfer is exact on the grid. You can train
coarse, then refine and keep training with more resolution. The paper calls
this _grid extension_, and it is one of the genuinely nice ideas in it.

Play with a single edge below. Drag the control points (they sit at the
_Greville abscissae_, the average of each basis function's interior knots,
which is where a spline's control polygon naturally lives), change the order,
add the SiLU term, refine the grid, and fit a target.

{cap="A single learnable KAN edge. The red curve is φ(x), the dashed polygon joins the B-spline coefficients c_m, and the strip below shows the basis functions B_m(x), with the one you are touching highlighted. Shaded regions are outside the grid: there the spline part decays to zero and only the SiLU base term survives. Try Fit sin(4x) at G = 5, then refine and fit again."}
::: widget-kan-spline
:::

### Counting parameters

Each edge has $G + k$ spline coefficients plus $w_b$, so a KAN layer has

$$
n_l \cdot n_{l+1} \cdot (G + k + 1)
$$

parameters, against $n_l \cdot n_{l+1} + n_{l+1}$ for a dense layer. With
$G = 5$ and $k = 3$ that is roughly **9 times** more parameters per
connection. For a stack of $L$ layers of width $N$, a KAN has
$O(N^2 L (G + k))$ parameters versus $O(N^2 L)$ for an MLP. The whole
argument of the paper is that KANs need a much smaller $N$ to reach the same
accuracy, so the comparison only makes sense at _matched parameter count_,
not matched width. That is what I do below.

Concretely, the $[2, 5, 1]$ KAN in the code has $15$ edges. At $G=5$ that is
$15 \times 9 = 135$ parameters, and after refining to $G = 10$ it is
$15 \times 14 = 210$. The MLP I compare against, $[2, 12, 12, 1]$ with tanh,
has $36 + 156 + 13 = 205$.

---

## A tiny KAN from scratch

Here is a complete KAN in NumPy: the Cox-de Boor basis (and its derivative), a
`KANLayer` with hand-written backprop, grid updates and refinement by least
squares, and a plain MLP with the same training loop. The target is the
example from the paper,

$$
f(x, y) = \exp\left(\sin(\pi x) + y^2\right),
$$

which a $[2, 1, 1]$ KAN can represent exactly: inner functions $\sin(\pi x)$
and $y^2$, with $\exp$ as the outer function. Both models train with full-batch Adam on
the same 500 points and are evaluated on 2000 fresh ones. A KAN step costs
about three times an MLP step, so the MLP gets three times as many steps,
which gives both models roughly the same compute budget. The gradients are
derived by hand (I checked them against finite differences). Press _Run_. It
takes about six seconds in the browser.

{.run}
```python
import time
import numpy as np

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)

# ---- data: f(x, y) = exp(sin(pi x) + y^2) on [-1, 1]^2 ----------------------
def target(X):
    return np.exp(np.sin(np.pi * X[:, 0]) + X[:, 1] ** 2)[:, None]

Xtr = rng.uniform(-1, 1, (500, 2)); ytr = target(Xtr)
Xte = rng.uniform(-1, 1, (2000, 2)); yte = target(Xte)

def silu(x):  return x / (1 + np.exp(-x))
def dsilu(x):
    s = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-x))
    return s * (1 + x * (1 - s))

# ---- B-splines (Cox-de Boor), vectorised over samples and inputs ------------
def knots(lo, hi, G, k):
    """One uniform grid per input: lo, hi have shape (d,). Returns (d, G+2k+1)."""
    h = (hi - lo) / G
    return lo[:, None] + h[:, None] * np.arange(-k, G + k + 1)

def bspline(x, t, k):
    """x: (N, d). Returns B (N, d, G+k) of order k and B' (its derivative)."""
    x = x[..., None]
    B = ((x >= t[:, :-1]) & (x < t[:, 1:])).astype(float)  # order 0
    for p in range(1, k + 1):
        if p == k:
            Bprev = B
        left = (x - t[:, :-(p + 1)]) / (t[:, p:-1] - t[:, :-(p + 1)])
        right = (t[:, p + 1:] - x) / (t[:, p + 1:] - t[:, 1:-p])
        B = left * B[..., :-1] + right * B[..., 1:]
    a = k / (t[:, k:-1] - t[:, :-(k + 1)])
    b = k / (t[:, k + 1:] - t[:, 1:-k])
    dB = a * Bprev[..., :-1] - b * Bprev[..., 1:]
    return B, dB

class KANLayer:
    """phi_ij(x) = wb_ij * silu(x) + sum_m c_ijm B_m(x), one per edge i -> j."""
    def __init__(self, d_in, d_out, G=5, k=3, lo=-1.0, hi=1.0):
        self.k, self.G = k, G
        self.t = knots(np.full(d_in, lo), np.full(d_in, hi), G, k)
        self.p = {
            "c": rng.normal(0, 0.1, (d_in, d_out, G + k)),
            "wb": rng.normal(0, 1 / np.sqrt(d_in), (d_in, d_out)),
        }

    def forward(self, x):
        if x is not getattr(self, "x", None):    # first layer: input never changes
            self.B, self.dB = bspline(x, self.t, self.k)
        self.x = x
        return silu(x) @ self.p["wb"] + np.einsum("nim,ijm->nj", self.B, self.p["c"])

    def backward(self, g):
        x = self.x
        self.g = {
            "c": np.einsum("nim,nj->ijm", self.B, g),
            "wb": silu(x).T @ g,
        }
        dphi = np.einsum("nim,ijm->nij", self.dB, self.p["c"])
        return (g @ self.p["wb"].T) * dsilu(x) + np.einsum("nj,nij->ni", g, dphi)

    def regrid(self, x, G):
        """Grid update / refinement: new grid on the data range, refit by lstsq."""
        lo, hi = x.min(0), x.max(0)
        pad = 0.1 * (hi - lo)
        lo, hi = lo - pad, hi + pad
        B, _ = bspline(x, self.t, self.k)
        old = np.einsum("nim,ijm->nij", B, self.p["c"])  # current spline part
        self.G, self.t = G, knots(lo, hi, G, self.k)
        self.x = None                             # invalidate cached basis
        Bn, _ = bspline(x, self.t, self.k)
        c = np.empty((x.shape[1], old.shape[2], G + self.k))
        for i in range(x.shape[1]):
            c[i] = np.linalg.lstsq(Bn[:, i, :], old[:, i, :], rcond=None)[0].T
        self.p["c"] = c

class Dense:
    def __init__(self, d_in, d_out, act):
        self.act = act
        self.p = {"W": rng.normal(0, 1 / np.sqrt(d_in), (d_in, d_out)),
                  "b": np.zeros(d_out)}
    def forward(self, x):
        self.x = x
        self.z = x @ self.p["W"] + self.p["b"]
        return np.tanh(self.z) if self.act else self.z
    def backward(self, g):
        if self.act:
            g = g * (1 - np.tanh(self.z) ** 2)
        self.g = {"W": self.x.T @ g, "b": g.sum(0)}
        return g @ self.p["W"].T

def n_params(layers):
    return sum(v.size for L in layers for v in L.p.values())

def predict(layers, x):
    for L in layers:
        x = L.forward(x)
    return x

def train(layers, steps, lr):
    """Full-batch Adam on MSE, gradients by hand-written backprop."""
    m = [{k: np.zeros_like(v) for k, v in L.p.items()} for L in layers]
    v = [{k: np.zeros_like(v) for k, v in L.p.items()} for L in layers]
    for s in range(1, steps + 1):
        err = predict(layers, Xtr) - ytr
        g = 2 * err / len(Xtr)
        for L in reversed(layers):
            g = L.backward(g)
        for L, mi, vi in zip(layers, m, v):
            for k in L.p:
                mi[k] = 0.9 * mi[k] + 0.1 * L.g[k]
                vi[k] = 0.999 * vi[k] + 0.001 * L.g[k] ** 2
                mh, vh = mi[k] / (1 - 0.9 ** s), vi[k] / (1 - 0.999 ** s)
                L.p[k] -= lr * mh / (np.sqrt(vh) + 1e-8)

def mse(layers, X, y):
    return float(np.mean((predict(layers, X) - y) ** 2))

# ---- KAN [2, 5, 1], cubic splines ------------------------------------------
kan = [KANLayer(2, 5, G=5), KANLayer(5, 1, G=5)]

p_kan_before = n_params(kan)
t_kan = time.time()
# Hidden activations wander outside the second layer's initial [-1, 1] grid,
# so every few hundred steps that grid is moved onto the range the data
# actually occupies. Halfway through, both grids are refined G = 5 -> 10 (the
# paper's "grid extension"). Each regrid refits the splines by least squares.
for phase in range(8):
    G = 5 if phase < 4 else 10
    if phase == 4:
        kan[0].regrid(Xtr, G)
    kan[1].regrid(kan[0].forward(Xtr), G)
    train(kan, 150, 1e-2)
p_kan = n_params(kan)
t_kan = time.time() - t_kan

# ---- MLP [2, H, H, 1] with tanh, sized to match the final KAN -------------
# A KAN step costs about 3x an MLP step, so the MLP gets 3x the steps:
# roughly the same wall-clock budget for both.
H = 12
mlp = [Dense(2, H, True), Dense(H, H, True), Dense(H, 1, False)]
t_mlp = time.time()
train(mlp, 3600, 1e-2)
t_mlp = time.time() - t_mlp

print("target: f(x,y) = exp(sin(pi x) + y^2), 500 train / 2000 test points")
print(f"KAN [2,5,1]  params {p_kan_before} -> {p_kan} (grid 5 -> 10), 1200 steps"
      f"   train MSE {mse(kan, Xtr, ytr):.2e}   test MSE {mse(kan, Xte, yte):.2e}   {t_kan:.1f}s")
print(f"MLP [2,{H},{H},1] params {n_params(mlp)}, 3600 steps"
      f"                     train MSE {mse(mlp, Xtr, ytr):.2e}   test MSE {mse(mlp, Xte, yte):.2e}   {t_mlp:.1f}s")
```

When I ran it in Pyodide, it printed:

```
KAN [2,5,1]  params 135 -> 210 (grid 5 -> 10), 1200 steps   train MSE 2.15e-06   test MSE 7.34e-06   3.0s
MLP [2,12,12,1] params 205, 3600 steps                     train MSE 1.39e-04   test MSE 3.11e-04   3.0s
```

(Native NumPy gives the same KAN numbers and an MLP test MSE of
$3.6 \times 10^{-4}$. The MLP's trajectory is sensitive enough that
floating-point differences between NumPy builds show up.)

So on this problem the KAN wins by more than an order of magnitude in test MSE
at roughly the same parameter count and compute. Changing the seed moves the
numbers around (KAN test MSE between about $7 \times 10^{-6}$ and
$4 \times 10^{-5}$, MLP between about $3 \times 10^{-4}$ and
$3 \times 10^{-3}$), but not the ordering. Widening the MLP to 337
parameters and training it for 10,000 steps (on 1000 training points) got it to about
$5 \times 10^{-5}$, which narrows the gap but does not close it. I also tried
a target without additive structure, $\sin(4xy)/(1+x^2)$, and the KAN still
came out ahead.

I want to be honest about what this does and doesn't show:

* **This is the KAN's home turf.** A smooth, two-dimensional function that is
  _literally_ a composition of univariate functions and a sum. It is the
  paper's own showcase. Low-dimensional smooth regression is where splines
  have always been excellent, KAN or not.
* **The KAN needed babysitting.** With a fixed grid, hidden activations drift
  outside the second layer's grid, where the splines vanish. In an early
  version of this script, some test points landed outside the grid and test
  MSE jumped to about $10^2$. The periodic grid updates are not optional
  decoration.
* **The KAN is slower.** Each step takes 3 to 4 times as long as an MLP
  step here, and that is with a vectorized basis on a tiny network. The MLP
  is a couple of matrix multiplies. At matched width, the KAN's matrix
  multiply is also $G + k + 1$ times larger, on top of evaluating the basis.

Edit the `target` function and try something without additive structure, or
add noise to `ytr`. It is the quickest way to form your own opinion.

---

## Where KANs stand

**Interpretability and symbolic regression.** This is the most distinctive
part of the paper. After training with a sparsity penalty, you prune unused
edges and look at each remaining $\phi$. If it looks like $\sin$ or $x^2$ or
$\exp$, you snap it to that symbolic form (fitting an affine
reparameterization) and continue training. On toy physics and math problems
this recovers exact formulas, which an MLP cannot give you so directly. The
follow-up, KAN 2.0, leans fully into this "AI for science" angle. It adds
multiplication nodes and tools for putting domain knowledge into KANs and
pulling formulas out. But this is interpretability of small networks on
low-dimensional, mostly symbolic problems. A KAN with thousands of edges is no
easier to read than an MLP.

**Fair comparisons.** Yu, Yu and Wang (2024) compared KANs and MLPs at matched
parameters and FLOPs across machine learning, vision, audio, NLP, and symbolic
formula tasks. MLPs generally won everywhere _except_ symbolic formula
representation. Their ablations attribute that advantage mainly to the B-spline
activation itself, and giving an MLP B-spline activations closed most of
that gap. They also found that KANs forget _more_ in class-incremental
continual learning, which contradicts one of the original paper's claims. My
toy result above is consistent with this: splines are great at smooth
low-dimensional functions.

**Training cost and speed.** The original paper is upfront that its KANs train
about 10 times slower than MLPs with the same number of parameters.
Evaluating a spline basis is not a single fused matrix multiply, and grid
updates add extra machinery to training. A lot of follow-up work is about
speed. FastKAN, for example, replaces the cubic B-splines with Gaussian
radial basis functions (they look very similar) and observes that the result
is a radial basis function network. That observation is a useful corrective in
itself: a lot of the KAN design space is old ideas (splines, basis expansions,
RBF networks, additive models) arranged in a new way.

**My take.** KANs are a genuinely nice idea for small scientific problems where
you want accuracy on smooth low-dimensional functions _and_ a formula at the
end. As a general replacement for MLPs, the evidence just isn't there yet, and
the original framing oversold it. That's fine. A good idea with an oversized
launch is still a good idea, and I expect the useful parts (learnable
activations, grid refinement, spline features) to survive in other
architectures.

---

## References

* Ziming Liu, Yixuan Wang, Sachin Vaidya, Fabian Ruehle, James Halverson, Marin Soljačić, Thomas Y. Hou, Max Tegmark. _KAN: Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks._ 2024. arXiv:2404.19756. <https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.19756>
* Ziming Liu, Pingchuan Ma, Yixuan Wang, Wojciech Matusik, Max Tegmark. _KAN 2.0: Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks Meet Science._ 2024. arXiv:2408.10205. <https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.10205>
* Runpeng Yu, Weihao Yu, Xinchao Wang. _KAN or MLP: A Fairer Comparison._ 2024. arXiv:2407.16674. <https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.16674>
* Ziyao Li. _Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks are Radial Basis Function Networks._ 2024. arXiv:2405.06721. <https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.06721>
* Johannes Schmidt-Hieber. _The Kolmogorov-Arnold representation theorem revisited._ 2020. arXiv:2007.15884. <https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.15884>
* Federico Girosi, Tomaso Poggio. _Representation Properties of Networks: Kolmogorov's Theorem Is Irrelevant._ Neural Computation 1(4), 1989.
* Andrey N. Kolmogorov. _On the representation of continuous functions of many variables by superposition of continuous functions of one variable and addition._ Doklady Akademii Nauk SSSR 114, 1957.
* Carl de Boor. _A Practical Guide to Splines._ Springer, 1978.
